## Claim
Text: "You’re watching the cheaters and all those people that send in the phony ballots. … They want to have the count weeks after November 3."
Claim author: Donald Trump
Claim date: October 30, 2020

## Initial Q&A
### Was there documented evidence of widespread voter fraud involving fake or "phony" ballots in the 2020 U.S. presidential election?
Answer: No, there was no documented evidence of widespread voter fraud involvingfake or "phony" ballots in the 2020 U.S. presidential election. The BBC article states that "numerous national and state-level studies show that voter fraud is incredibly rare in the US" and that the Trump campaign "often claimed, without evidence" that mail-in voting would lead to tremendous fraud. It also quotes Ellen Weintraub, commissioner of the Federal Election Commission, who said: "There's simply no basis for the conspiracy theory that voting by mail causes fraud."

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/54562611

### Did election officials in the 2020 U.S. presidential election identify significant numbers of fraudulent ballots that would support Donald Trump's claim of "cheaters" sending in "phony" ballots?
Answer: No. The search result indicates that "numerous national and state-level studies show that voter fraud is incredibly rare in the US," and Ellen Weintraub, commissioner of the Federal Election Commission, stated that "there's simply no basis for the conspiracy theory that voting by mail causes fraud." The BBC notes that Trump's claims of widespread fraud were made "without evidence," and while there were isolated cases (such as nine discarded military ballots in Pennsylvania under FBI investigation), election officials did not identify significant numbers of fraudulent ballots that would support Trump's characterization of "cheaters" sending in "phony" ballots.

Source URL: https://www.bbc.com/news/54562611

### Is it legal and standard procedure in multiple U.S. states for mail-in ballots postmarked by Election Day to be counted in the days and weeks following November 3, 2020?
Answer: Yes. According to USA Today's fact check, "21 states and Washington, D.C., will count ballots that arrive after Nov. 3, with deadlines varying." The article details numerous examples, including Illinois (ballots must be postmarked by Election Day and received up to Nov. 17), California (postmarked by Nov. 3, returned no later than Nov. 20), Washington state (postmarked Nov. 3, received by Nov. 23), North Carolina (postmarked by Nov. 3, received by Nov. 12), and several others where ballots postmarked by Election Day were legally accepted and counted days or even weeks after November 3, 2020.

Source URL: https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/factcheck/2020/10/30/fact-check-some-mail-ballots-arriving-post-election-day-count/6075311002/

### Did the continuation of vote counting after November 3, 2020, in the U.S. presidential election involve ballots that were legally cast and verified by election officials?
Answer: Yes, the continuation of vote counting after November 3, 2020, involved ballots that were legally cast and verified by election officials. According to the Bipartisan Policy Center report, "Before a mail ballot can be counted, election officials must first confirm the voter's identity and eligibility to vote" through processes such as signature verification, and "There is nothing objectively wrong with a slow vote counting process—many states with extensive mail voting are accustomed to a long period for counting, canvassing, auditing, and certifying election results." The report explains that the unprecedented volume of mail-in ballots (estimated at 50-70% of all ballots cast due to COVID-19) required additional time for legitimate processing, and that "election results are often not made official until weeks after Election Day, when election administrators complete an official canvass and certify the results." Trump's characterization of these ballots as "phony" and the voters as "cheaters" is contradicted by the documented verification procedures that election officials followed.

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/

### Did any post-election audits, recounts, or investigations in the 2020 U.S. presidential election find evidence of widespread voter fraud that would substantiate Donald Trump's claims?
Answer: No. The search result from the Georgia Secretary of State documents only 35 individual cases referred for prosecution—including four cases of felons voting, four cases of non-citizens voting, and one case of "misplaced ballots during the 2020 general election, which didn't change the outcome." This handful of isolated cases among millions of votes cast does not constitute evidence of the widespread fraud Trump alleged, and the source itself explicitly states the misplaced ballots case "didn't change the outcome."

Source URL: https://sos.ga.gov/news/state-election-board-refers-voter-fraud-cases-prosecution-0

