### Was the delay in counting votes after November 3, 2020, primarily caused by the high volume of mail-in ballots cast during the COVID-19 pandemic rather than by fraudulent activity?
Answer: Yes, according to the Bipartisan Policy Center report, the delay in counting votes after November 3, 2020, was primarily caused by the unprecedented surge in mail-in ballots due to the COVID-19 pandemic, not by fraudulent activity. The report states that "The coronavirus pandemic has greatly disrupted the 2020 U.S. election. One consequence, unusually slow results reporting, poses a serious threat to the election's legitimacy," and explains that "Counting the vote and results reporting will take longer than usual this year for a variety of reasons. Notably, there will be a massive increase in voting by mail," with BPC estimating "50%-70% of all ballots will be cast absentee—up from less than 25% nationally in 2018." It further notes that "In many states, election systems are simply not set up to accommodate the expected increase in people voting by mail, and election officials will be overwhelmed," and that "reviewing, processing, and tallying ballots in most states is a time-intensive and often manual process." The report explicitly warns that without understanding these issues, "many may unwittingly spread misinformation or make unfounded claims of fraud or misconduct that will harm voter confidence."

Source URL: https://bipartisanpolicy.org/report/counting-the-vote-during-the-2020-election/

### Did Donald Trump or his legal team ever present specific, verified evidence in court or to election officials proving that "phony" ballots were sent by "cheaters" in the 2020 U.S. presidential election?
Answer: Based on the search result, in at least one documented case, the Trump campaign failed to produce specific, verified evidence of vote-by-mail fraud when ordered to do so by a federal court. As The Guardian reported, U.S. District Judge Nicholas Ranjan (a Trump appointee) ordered the campaign to turn over its evidence of fraud in Pennsylvania, but the campaign "turned over little evidence of pervasive fraud" and "offered no evidence of fraud specifically linked to dropboxes or mail-in ballots," instead submitting a "half-dozen news articles" and public records requests. An opposing attorney described the evidence as "paltry," noting the campaign "has not provided any meaningful evidence that voter fraud is a widespread problem in Pennsylvania." However, this search result only covers a pre-election lawsuit from August 2020 and does not comprehensively address all post-election legal proceedings or claims made after November 3, 2020, so a more thorough review of post-election litigation would be needed to fully answer the question.

Source URL: https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/aug/21/trump-campaign-voter-fraud-pennsylvania

## Final Judgement
## Summary

The evidence gathered through the fact-check overwhelmingly and consistently refutes both components of Trump's claim. First, regarding "cheaters" sending in "phony ballots," multiple authoritative sources confirm there was no documented evidence of widespread voter fraud in the 2020 election. The BBC cited studies showing voter fraud is "incredibly rare," the FBI director stated there was "no evidence of widespread fraud," and FEC Commissioner Weintraub said "there's simply no basis for the conspiracy theory that voting by mail causes fraud." Post-election investigations in Georgia found only 35 individual cases among millions of votes cast, and Trump's own legal team failed to produce meaningful evidence of fraud when ordered by a federal court, offering what an opposing attorney called "paltry" evidence. Second, regarding the claim that counting "weeks after November 3" is suspicious, the evidence shows this was entirely legal and standard practice—21 states and Washington, D.C. legally accepted and counted mail-in ballots postmarked by Election Day for days or weeks afterward, and the delays were caused by the unprecedented volume of mail-in ballots (50-70% of all ballots) due to the COVID-19 pandemic, not by fraudulent activity.

## Missing Information

The evidence is comprehensive and consistent across multiple independent sources. No further investigation is needed to reach a clear conclusion.

## Conclusion

The claim is clearly refuted by the evidence. The characterization of "cheaters" sending "phony ballots" lacks any substantive supporting evidence, as confirmed by election officials, federal agencies, and even courts where Trump himself had to present evidence. The suggestion that counting ballots weeks after November 3 is inherently suspicious is also refuted, as this was standard legal procedure in numerous states, necessitated by the unprecedented surge in mail-in voting during the COVID-19 pandemic. Both parts of the claim are contradicted by the documented record.

